T = []
def tk(id, level, voice, title, text, qs): T.append({"id": id, "level": level, "voice": voice, "title": title, "text": text, "questions": qs})
tk("tk08",1,"M","Why We Have Leap Years","Have you ever wondered why February sometimes has twenty-nine days? It's because of something called a leap year. The Earth takes about three hundred sixty-five and a quarter days to travel around the sun. But our calendar year has only three hundred sixty-five days. So every year, the calendar falls about six hours behind. After four years, those extra hours add up to almost one full day. To fix this, we add an extra day, February twenty-ninth, every four years. Without leap years, our seasons would slowly move. After a few hundred years, summer would start in what we now call autumn.",[
 mc("What is the talk mainly about?","Why leap years exist",["How the seasons change","The history of February","How long a day is"],"Thema im ersten Satz."),
 mc("How long does the Earth take to travel around the sun?","About 365 and a quarter days",["Exactly 365 days","366 days","About 360 days"],"„about three hundred sixty-five and a quarter days“."),
 mc("How often is an extra day added?","Every four years",["Every year","Every two years","Every hundred years"],"„every four years“."),
 mc("What would happen without leap years?","The seasons would slowly shift in the calendar.",["February would disappear.","The Earth would move faster.","Days would get longer."],"Der Sommer würde irgendwann im heutigen Herbst beginnen.")])
tk("tk09",1,"W","How Bees Make Honey","Today let's talk about how bees make honey. First, worker bees fly from flower to flower and collect a sweet liquid called nectar. They store the nectar in a special stomach and carry it back to the hive. There, they pass the nectar to other bees, who chew it for about half an hour. Then the bees put it into small wax cells. The nectar is still very watery, so the bees fan it with their wings to dry it. When most of the water is gone, the nectar has become honey. Finally, the bees cover each cell with wax to keep the honey clean. One bee makes only about a twelfth of a teaspoon of honey in its whole life.",[
 mc("What do bees collect from flowers?","Nectar",["Wax","Honey","Water"],"„a sweet liquid called nectar“."),
 mc("Why do bees fan the nectar with their wings?","To remove water from it",["To keep it warm","To make it sweeter","To move it to another hive"],"„to dry it“."),
 mc("What do the bees do last?","Cover the cells with wax",["Chew the nectar","Fly to more flowers","Fan the honey"],"„Finally, the bees cover each cell with wax“."),
 mc("What does the speaker say about a single bee?","It produces only a small amount of honey in its life.",["It can visit every flower in one day.","It lives for many years.","It makes a teaspoon of honey every day."],"Nur etwa 1/12 Teelöffel im ganzen Leben.")])
tk("tk10",2,"M","The Bystander Effect","In psychology, the bystander effect refers to a surprising finding: people are less likely to help someone in an emergency when other people are present. You might expect the opposite, that more witnesses would mean more help. But in a series of experiments in the 1960s, researchers found that participants who believed they were the only person hearing someone in distress helped quickly, while those who thought several others could hear often did nothing. Researchers point to two main causes. The first is diffusion of responsibility: when many people are present, each person feels less personally responsible. The second is social influence. In an unclear situation, people look at how others react, and if no one else seems worried, they assume there's no emergency. The good news is that simply knowing about the effect can reduce it. And if you ever need help in a crowd, experts suggest pointing to one specific person and asking them directly.",[
 mc("What is the bystander effect?","People are less likely to help when others are present.",["People help more when others are watching.","People panic in emergencies.","People avoid crowded places."],"Definition am Anfang."),
 mc("What did the experiments in the 1960s show?","People who thought they were alone helped more quickly.",["Most participants refused to help anyone.","Groups helped faster than individuals.","Participants could not hear the person in distress."],"Allein → schnell geholfen; mit anderen → oft nichts."),
 mc("According to the speaker, what does diffusion of responsibility mean?","Each person feels less responsible when many people are present.",["People share tasks equally.","Responsibility increases in larger groups.","One leader takes control of the situation."],"Verantwortung verteilt sich."),
 mc("What does the speaker recommend if you need help in a crowd?","Ask one specific person directly",["Shout as loudly as possible","Wait for someone to notice","Call several people at once"],"„pointing to one specific person and asking them directly“.")])
tk("tk11",3,"W","The Columbian Exchange","Historians use the term Columbian Exchange to describe the massive transfer of plants, animals, and diseases between the Americas and the rest of the world that followed 1492. Its consequences are difficult to overstate. Crops native to the Americas, such as potatoes, maize, and tomatoes, eventually transformed diets across Europe, Africa, and Asia. The potato, in particular, could produce more calories per acre than traditional European grains, and some scholars argue it contributed substantially to population growth in eighteenth-century Europe. The exchange flowed in the other direction as well. Europeans introduced horses, cattle, and wheat to the Americas, reshaping indigenous economies and ways of life. Horses, for instance, enabled some Plains peoples to adopt a highly mobile, bison-hunting culture. Yet the most devastating consequence was biological. Diseases such as smallpox, to which indigenous populations had no prior exposure, spread with catastrophic speed, killing a large proportion of the population in many regions, often before the newcomers themselves had arrived in those areas.",[
 mc("What is the main topic of the talk?","The transfer of plants, animals, and diseases after 1492 and its effects",["The life of Christopher Columbus","How potatoes are grown","European trade routes to Asia"],"Begriff und Folgen des Columbian Exchange."),
 mc("Why does the speaker mention the potato?","To illustrate how an American crop changed life elsewhere",["To explain the origin of European grains","To show that Europeans rejected new foods","To describe what Plains peoples hunted"],"Mehr Kalorien pro Acre → Bevölkerungswachstum."),
 mc("According to the speaker, how did horses affect some Plains peoples?","They made a mobile, bison-hunting way of life possible.",["They replaced farming in Europe.","They spread disease.","They were used mainly for trade with Asia."],"„enabled some Plains peoples to adopt a highly mobile, bison-hunting culture“."),
 mc("What does the speaker imply about the spread of disease?","It sometimes moved faster than the Europeans themselves.",["It affected Europeans more than indigenous peoples.","It was planned deliberately.","It had little effect on population size."],"„often before the newcomers themselves had arrived“.")])
tk("tk12",3,"M","Opportunity Cost","One of the most useful concepts in economics is also one of the most frequently misunderstood: opportunity cost. The opportunity cost of a choice isn't the money you spend; it's the value of the best alternative you give up. Suppose you spend a Saturday working a shift that pays a hundred dollars instead of going to a friend's concert. The opportunity cost of working is the concert, and the opportunity cost of the concert is the hundred dollars you'd have earned. This framing has some counterintuitive implications. Consider a student deciding whether to attend graduate school. Tuition is obvious, but the larger cost is often invisible: the salary the student would have earned during those years. Economists argue that ignoring these hidden costs leads people to make decisions that look sensible on paper but aren't. The concept also explains why time becomes more valuable as income rises. A highly paid professional who spends an afternoon repainting her own house may, in economic terms, be paying far more than if she'd hired someone, even though no money changes hands.",[
 mc("What is opportunity cost?","The value of the best alternative that is given up",["The money spent on a purchase","The total cost of all available options","The profit gained from a decision"],"Definition im zweiten Satz."),
 mc("Why does the speaker mention graduate school?","To show that a major cost may be income one does not earn",["To argue that tuition is too expensive","To recommend working before studying","To explain how salaries are calculated"],"Das entgangene Gehalt ist der größere, unsichtbare Kostenpunkt."),
 mc("According to the speaker, what happens when people ignore hidden costs?","They may make poor decisions that seem reasonable.",["They save more money.","They always choose the cheaper option.","They avoid making decisions."],"„decisions that look sensible on paper but aren't“."),
 mc("What does the speaker imply about the professional who paints her own house?","Her time may be worth more than the cost of hiring a painter.",["She saves money by doing it herself.","She enjoys painting more than her job.","She should paint more often."],"Ökonomisch zahlt sie mehr, obwohl kein Geld fließt.")])
append("listening", "talk", T)
